title: Benchmark Algorithms – I

files:
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/MatrixBench.em.ts
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/StateBench.em.ts
  - .assets/img-102-03-01.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]Algorithms – %[cu]MatrixBench% &middot; %[cu]StateBench%}

      We've finally arrived at the heart of **CoreMark** &ndash; its trio of bench&shy;mark algorithms.

      This tour focuses on the *matrix-math* and *state-machine* algorithms embodied in {[cu]MatrixBench} and {[cu]StateBench}.

      Like {[cu]CoreBench} seen previously, each of these modules implements a common {[cu]BenchAlgI} interface.

      {[in]We'll highlight their %[cf]run% and %[cf]setup% functions, which track legacy code found in %[cx]core_&lbrace;matrix,state&rbrace;.c% }

  - cmds:
      - close 3
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 7
      - mark 1 B 14
      - mark 1 C 26
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]%[cu]MatrixBench% &ndash; %[cx]em$meta% details}

      {[cu]MatrixBench} subjects the three {[cs]$table} objects declared at line {[bm,B]} to a variety of matrix-math opertions.

      The value of {[cn]mem_size}{[bm,A]} (bound by {[cu]CoreBench} at build-time) ultimately determines the dimension of {[cn]mat&lbrack;ABC&rbrack;}.

      A calculation inside {[cs]em$construct} eventually binds this dimension value to the {[cn]dimN} config parameter at line {[bm,C]}.

      {[in]The subsequent loop then dimensions and zeroes the %[cs]$table% matricies using the intrinsic <b>%[cx]$$add%</b> operator.}

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 51]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]%[cu]MatrixBench% &ndash; %[cf]run% details}

      Starting with its scalar argument, {[cu]MatrixBench}.{[cf]run} ma&shy;nip&shy;ulates {[cn]mat&lbrack;ABC&rbrack;} through a series of private functions.

      {[in]Navigate to helpers like %[cf]addVal% and %[cf]mulVec%, which match functions found in the legacy <b>CoreMark</b> code.}

      To compute its {[ct]sum_t} result, {[cu]MatrixBench}.{[cf]run} updates its local {[cn]crc} variable with a scalar returned by {[cf]sumDat}.

      The {[ct]sum_t} returned by {[cf]run} folds the accumulated {[cn]crc} value into the benchmark's {[cn]FINAL} checksum.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 70
    focus: [1, 73]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]%[cu]MatrixBench% &ndash; %[cf]setup% details}

      The {[cu]MatrixBench}.{[cf]setup} initializes (at *run-time*) the {[cn]matA} and {[cn]matB} objects dimensioned and zeroed at *build-time*.

      The scalar values held in these matricies ultimately derive from special **CoreMark** seed values read here at line {[bm,A]}.

      {[in]Recall that %[cu]UT%.%[cf]getSeed% forcibly reads (at <i>run-time</i>) an unknown constant value bound at <i>build-time</i>.}

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,fence]%[cu]BenchAlgI%.%[cf]setup% &ndash; domain boundaries}

      The {[cf]setup} function we just examined could seem&shy;ingly have resided in the **`em$meta`** domain.

      {[in]Build-time initialization of complex data structures in fact distinguishes <b>EMS</b> from other embedded languages.}

      Moving **`em$targ`** code upstream to the **`em$meta`** domain reduces program size and often improves performance.

      But **CoreMark** forbids this &ndash; fearing a compiler might "pre-compute" benchmark results from static datasets.

      **CoreMark** thus requires *explicit* memory reads of all seed values &ndash; like any program input known only at run-time.

      {[in]Our <b>EMS</b> modules will maximize build-time initial&shy;ization, while still respecting <b>CoreMark</b>'s inherent constraint.}

  - cmds:
      - open 2
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]%[cu]StateBench% &ndash; %[cx]em$meta% details}

  - cmds:
      - open 2
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]%[cu]StateBench% &ndash; %[cf]run% details}

  - cmds:
      - open 2
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]%[cu]StateBench% &ndash; %[cf]setup% details}

  - cmds:
      - close 2
      - close 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Compare {[cu]MatrixBench} with {[cx]core_matrix.c}.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Compare {[cu]StateBench} with {[cx]core_state.c}.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Trace one {[ct]sum_t} value returned from {[cf]run}.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Identify setup work that depends on runtime seeds.

  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |
      {[ht,$done]Next Steps}

      {[hc]}
